1
00:00:07,799 --> 00:00:13,705
In the spring of twenty twenty-eight, Umbrella Life
Sciences had three point two billion

2
00:00:13,725 --> 00:00:19,631
dollars in the bank, a drug the whole world wanted,
and a problem that money alone could

3
00:00:19,651 --> 00:00:20,304
not solve.

4
00:00:20,875 --> 00:00:24,342
It did not have enough people who were
better than everyone else.

5
00:00:24,911 --> 00:00:27,062
So Albert Wesker went and got them.

6
00:00:28,431 --> 00:00:34,653
He had a list. Pell had built it — forty names, drawn
from every university and laboratory

7
00:00:34,673 --> 00:00:40,964
on Earth that did the kinds of work Umbrella would
need over the next ten years. Structural

8
00:00:40,984 --> 00:00:47,066
biology. Medicinal chemistry. Protein design.
Immunology. Neuroscience. Forty people who

9
00:00:47,086 --> 00:00:52,059
were, by the measure of their peers, the best
in the world at one thing.

10
00:00:52,630 --> 00:00:58,801
He met twenty-six of them himself. He recruited
twenty-two. He did it in fourteen weeks,

11
00:00:58,821 --> 00:01:05,414
on four continents, and he used, depending on the
person, one of three tools. Money. Flattery.

12
00:01:05,434 --> 00:01:06,681
And the third one.

13
00:01:08,751 --> 00:01:10,585
At Oxford, it was flattery.

14
00:01:11,155 --> 00:01:17,050
Professor Imogen Carter had spent thirty years
mapping the shapes of proteins, and had

15
00:01:17,070 --> 00:01:23,171
been passed over twice for the prize she deserved,
both times in favour of men. He walked

16
00:01:23,191 --> 00:01:29,361
with her around a fifteenth-century cloister for an
hour, and did not once mention salary.

17
00:01:29,381 --> 00:01:35,276
He talked about her second paper — not her famous
one, the second — and about an error

18
00:01:35,296 --> 00:01:41,810
in its fourth figure that nobody else had ever noticed,
including the reviewers, and including,

19
00:01:41,830 --> 00:01:44,286
until that afternoon, Imogen Carter.

20
00:01:44,977 --> 00:01:49,074
You came here to tell me I made a mistake
in nineteen ninety-six?

21
00:01:49,594 --> 00:01:54,976
I came here to tell you that I've read everything
you've ever written closely enough to

22
00:01:54,996 --> 00:01:59,571
find it. Nobody else ever has. Not the
committee. Not your department. Me.

23
00:02:00,891 --> 00:02:06,312
She came to Wilmington in September. She never did
work out whether he had meant it as

24
00:02:06,332 --> 00:02:07,135
a compliment.

25
00:02:08,905 --> 00:02:10,596
In Basel, it was money.

26
00:02:11,165 --> 00:02:17,056
Doctor Lukas Ammann was thirty-four, brilliant,
arrogant, and deeply in debt from a divorce

27
00:02:17,076 --> 00:02:22,578
and a start-up that had failed. He negotiated for
two days. He was good at it. On the

28
00:02:22,598 --> 00:02:28,294
third morning, Wesker slid a single sheet of paper
across the table with a number on it,

29
00:02:28,314 --> 00:02:34,010
and Lukas Ammann stopped negotiating, and looked at
the number for a long time, and then

30
00:02:34,030 --> 00:02:37,062
laughed — a short, helpless
laugh — and signed.

31
00:02:38,832 --> 00:02:45,137
In Singapore, it was curiosity, which is a kind of
flattery. Doctor Tan Wei Lin, twenty-nine,

32
00:02:45,157 --> 00:02:50,985
had designed a synthetic bacterium that could build
a molecule nobody else could make,

33
00:02:51,005 --> 00:02:56,901
and had been told by her institute that it had no
commercial application. He showed her

34
00:02:56,921 --> 00:03:02,817
one page of the Arklay budget. She asked three
questions. He answered one. She followed

35
00:03:02,837 --> 00:03:05,129
him to Delaware for the other two.

36
00:03:06,899 --> 00:03:09,451
And in Scotland, it was the third tool.

37
00:03:10,022 --> 00:03:15,886
Sir Martin Ellery was seventy-one years old, a Nobel
laureate in medicine, a member of

38
00:03:15,906 --> 00:03:21,906
every learned society worth belonging to, and the
most famous immunologist alive. He had

39
00:03:21,926 --> 00:03:27,995
been approached by every biotech company on Earth. He
had turned them all down. In March,

40
00:03:28,015 --> 00:03:33,878
a week after the bell in Times Square, he received
Albert Wesker in the library of his

41
00:03:33,898 --> 00:03:39,077
house in Perthshire, gave him a cup of tea and
fifteen minutes, and said no.

42
00:03:39,647 --> 00:03:45,128
He said no very politely. He said he was too old,
and too tired, and too comfortable.

43
00:03:45,148 --> 00:03:50,822
He said that he had had a long career and a good one,
and that he intended to spend what

44
00:03:50,842 --> 00:03:54,252
remained of it walking his dogs and
reading Trollope.

45
00:03:54,822 --> 00:03:57,848
The Director thanked him, and
finished his tea, and left.

46
00:03:58,419 --> 00:04:03,943
But on the way to the door, he had watched Sir Martin
reach for his cup, and watched his

47
00:04:03,963 --> 00:04:09,362
right hand — the Cold-Read noting it with total
precision — hesitate, and correct, and

48
00:04:09,382 --> 00:04:14,780
hesitate again. A tremor too fine for anyone else to
see. A tiny weakness in the grip.

49
00:04:14,800 --> 00:04:17,930
A faint thickening in the speech,
on the letter L.

50
00:04:19,500 --> 00:04:25,252
He did not say anything. He went back to his car and
made a telephone call to a private

51
00:04:25,272 --> 00:04:30,957
neurologist in Edinburgh, and arranged, through a
foundation Pell had established that

52
00:04:30,977 --> 00:04:36,595
morning, for Sir Martin Ellery to be offered a
routine check-up as part of a study of

53
00:04:36,615 --> 00:04:38,254
distinguished scientists.

54
00:04:38,824 --> 00:04:45,338
In April, the neurologist gave Sir Martin his
diagnosis. Motor neurone disease. Eighteen

55
00:04:45,358 --> 00:04:47,269
months, perhaps two years.

56
00:04:47,838 --> 00:04:50,167
In May, Sir Martin called Wilmington.

57
00:04:51,857 --> 00:04:55,341
Doctor Wesker. I believe I may
have been hasty.

58
00:04:55,860 --> 00:04:59,062
I don't believe you were ever hasty in
your life, Sir Martin.

59
00:04:59,583 --> 00:05:05,655
No. I suppose I've simply become frightened. Is it
true — the things they say about your

60
00:05:05,675 --> 00:05:06,279
compound?

61
00:05:06,798 --> 00:05:09,081
Come to Delaware. I'll show you
what's true.

62
00:05:11,101 --> 00:05:16,944
It is important to be precise about what happened
here, because Albert Wesker was always

63
00:05:16,964 --> 00:05:22,607
precise about it himself. He did not give Sir
Martin Ellery a disease. He did not lie

64
00:05:22,627 --> 00:05:28,336
to him. He simply noticed it first, and arranged for
the man to find out at the moment

65
00:05:28,356 --> 00:05:33,999
most useful to Umbrella, and then offered him the
one thing no other company on Earth

66
00:05:34,019 --> 00:05:34,398
could.

67
00:05:34,970 --> 00:05:35,731
Time.

68
00:05:36,420 --> 00:05:38,297
That was well done.

69
00:05:38,817 --> 00:05:39,946
It was a kindness.

70
00:05:40,466 --> 00:05:43,204
Yes. That is what made it well done.

71
00:05:45,225 --> 00:05:50,295
The twenty-two humans came from outside. The
twelve others came from the System.

72
00:05:51,615 --> 00:05:54,874
Purchase confirmed. Research cohort.

73
00:05:58,889 --> 00:06:04,877
They arrived on the same Monday, in the same way
that Pell had arrived, and Hale, and

74
00:06:04,897 --> 00:06:11,027
Gus — already hired, already credentialed, with
degrees from real universities that had

75
00:06:11,047 --> 00:06:17,317
real records of them, and publication histories in
real journals that nobody in the field

76
00:06:17,337 --> 00:06:19,508
had ever quite managed to read.

77
00:06:20,077 --> 00:06:25,577
They were very good. They were better than good. They
were, as far as anyone could tell,

78
00:06:25,597 --> 00:06:30,909
exactly as good as the best person on Earth at
whatever they had been assigned to do,

79
00:06:30,929 --> 00:06:35,237
and they could do it for twenty hours a day
without making a mistake.

80
00:06:35,809 --> 00:06:39,867
They did not go home. That was the first
thing the humans noticed.

81
00:06:40,435 --> 00:06:45,931
Tan Wei Lin noticed it in her second week, when she
came in at five in the morning to

82
00:06:45,951 --> 00:06:51,446
check a culture and found three of them at the same
benches they had been at when she

83
00:06:51,466 --> 00:06:55,989
left at midnight, in the same positions, as
though they had not moved.

84
00:06:56,560 --> 00:07:01,788
The second thing the humans noticed was that none of
them had ever been to a conference.

85
00:07:02,478 --> 00:07:06,728
Where did your team train, Evelyn? I've looked them
up. They have papers, but I can't

86
00:07:06,748 --> 00:07:11,049
find anyone who's ever met them. Not one talk. Not
one poster. Not one person who went

87
00:07:11,069 --> 00:07:12,054
to school with them.

88
00:07:12,574 --> 00:07:13,723
They're private sector.

89
00:07:14,242 --> 00:07:16,495
Private sector people go to conferences.

90
00:07:17,014 --> 00:07:18,477
These ones don't need to.

91
00:07:19,997 --> 00:07:25,942
Tan Wei Lin let it go. She had been given a
laboratory, a budget with no ceiling, and

92
00:07:25,962 --> 00:07:31,908
a problem that nobody else on Earth was working on.
She was twenty-nine, and she told

93
00:07:31,928 --> 00:07:37,523
herself it did not matter who her colleagues
were, as long as the work was real.

94
00:07:38,093 --> 00:07:40,010
This is also how it happens.

95
00:07:42,199 --> 00:07:46,538
They are perfect. A civil service that
cannot be corrupted.

96
00:07:47,057 --> 00:07:52,410
They're the first employees in history who can't be
poached. That's all I need them to

97
00:07:52,430 --> 00:07:52,598
be.

98
00:07:53,119 --> 00:07:55,275
You understate everything.

99
00:07:55,795 --> 00:07:59,398
You overstate everything. Between us,
we're roughly accurate.

100
00:08:01,416 --> 00:08:05,075
He did the draw on the jet, over the
Atlantic, at night.

101
00:08:05,646 --> 00:08:11,595
The jet was Catherine's doing. She had bought it in
April, arguing that a chief executive

102
00:08:11,615 --> 00:08:17,630
who spent a third of his life in airports was a poor
use of a three-billion-dollar balance

103
00:08:17,650 --> 00:08:18,033
sheet.

104
00:08:19,353 --> 00:08:20,407
Ten draws.

105
00:08:27,218 --> 00:08:28,315
Results.

106
00:08:38,273 --> 00:08:39,105
Jet lag.

107
00:08:39,625 --> 00:08:41,504
Fate is mocking you.

108
00:08:42,024 --> 00:08:47,815
Fate has just made me the only chief executive in
the world who can land in Singapore

109
00:08:47,835 --> 00:08:52,463
and hold a board meeting. It isn't mocking
me. It's being practical.

110
00:08:55,714 --> 00:08:57,583
Epic. Thought acceleration.

111
00:09:03,813 --> 00:09:07,828
He tried it immediately. It would have been
out of character not to.

112
00:09:08,399 --> 00:09:13,977
There was a raindrop on the window of the jet. It
had been running backward along the

113
00:09:13,997 --> 00:09:17,665
glass, driven by the wind at five
hundred miles an hour.

114
00:09:18,235 --> 00:09:19,255
It stopped.

115
00:09:19,826 --> 00:09:25,158
Not really, of course. It was still moving. But
inside his skull, a single second had

116
00:09:25,178 --> 00:09:30,825
been stretched out into a minute, and the raindrop was
crawling across the glass so slowly

117
00:09:30,845 --> 00:09:36,241
that he could see the light of the moon inside it,
bending, and the tiny tremor in its

118
00:09:36,261 --> 00:09:41,593
surface where the air was tearing at it, and the
path it was going to take across the

119
00:09:41,613 --> 00:09:44,552
window before it reached the
edge and vanished.

120
00:09:45,123 --> 00:09:50,474
He watched it for what felt like a minute. He
thought about the recruitment list, and

121
00:09:50,494 --> 00:09:56,034
the Arklay budget, and a woman in Manhattan who knew
he was lying and did not know which

122
00:09:56,054 --> 00:10:01,531
part. He thought about a sealed black card that said
not yet. He thought, very clearly,

123
00:10:01,551 --> 00:10:05,892
through six separate ways the next two
years could go, and chose one.

124
00:10:06,461 --> 00:10:07,682
Then he let go.

125
00:10:09,052 --> 00:10:14,522
The raindrop streaked across the glass and was gone.
On the other side of the cabin, his

126
00:10:14,542 --> 00:10:19,949
new executive assistant — who had appeared in the
rear seat of the jet at the moment of

127
00:10:19,969 --> 00:10:25,376
the draw, already dressed, already holding a tablet
with his schedule on it — looked up

128
00:10:25,396 --> 00:10:27,934
and asked whether he would like a coffee.

129
00:10:28,504 --> 00:10:32,652
He said yes. It had been,
subjectively, a very long second.

130
00:10:34,841 --> 00:10:37,477
Do you understand what this is?

131
00:10:37,998 --> 00:10:39,109
A useful tool.

132
00:10:39,629 --> 00:10:45,707
It is time. It is the only thing I never had enough
of. In the last year I needed a day

133
00:10:45,727 --> 00:10:51,805
for every hour, and I had nothing. And you have been
given the one thing that every man

134
00:10:51,825 --> 00:10:54,820
who ever ruled anything has
begged God for.

135
00:10:55,339 --> 00:10:59,365
You didn't lose for lack of time. You
lost for lack of judgment.

136
00:10:59,884 --> 00:11:02,168
And now we have both.

137
00:11:04,188 --> 00:11:10,158
Sir Martin Ellery arrived in Wilmington in June,
with his dogs, and a cane he did not

138
00:11:10,178 --> 00:11:15,161
want, and a tremor in his right hand that
was no longer quite so small.

139
00:11:15,731 --> 00:11:18,571
On his third day, the Director
took him down.

140
00:11:19,140 --> 00:11:24,588
He walked the white corridor slowly, on the cane,
looking into every laboratory the way

141
00:11:24,608 --> 00:11:30,055
a man looks into the windows of a house he used to
live in. He did not speak until they

142
00:11:30,075 --> 00:11:35,585
reached the cold room. And then he stood in front of
the viewing window for a long time,

143
00:11:35,605 --> 00:11:41,429
looking at the thing that slept inside, and his old
face went through a number of expressions

144
00:11:41,449 --> 00:11:44,572
that the Cold-Read had to work
quite hard to name.

145
00:11:46,261 --> 00:11:47,574
Dear God.

146
00:11:48,095 --> 00:11:49,866
That was the first thing he said.

147
00:11:51,936 --> 00:11:57,876
He looked at it for another minute. He looked at the
four research associates in the laboratory

148
00:11:57,896 --> 00:12:03,334
behind him, who did not look up. He looked at Evelyn
Marsh, standing at the edge of the

149
00:12:03,354 --> 00:12:08,729
room with her hands clasped in front of her like a
girl at a recital. And he looked at

150
00:12:08,749 --> 00:12:12,745
the man in the black coat beside him, who
was offering him time.

151
00:12:14,434 --> 00:12:16,031
When do I start?
